<h1>Rainbow Tables Explained and Why Salts Matter</h1>
<p>Rainbow tables are precomputed data structures used to speed up password hash lookup. The basic idea is simple: calculate hashes for many possible passwords ahead of time, then use that work later when a hash database is leaked. They are one reason unsalted password hashes are dangerous.</p>
<p>A normal lookup table might store password to hash pairs. A rainbow table uses a more space-efficient method involving chains and reduction functions, but the practical goal is similar: avoid starting from zero for every target hash. If many systems use the same fast unsalted hash method, precomputation becomes valuable.</p>
<p>Salts reduce that value. OWASP explains that a unique salt makes attackers crack hashes one at a time and protects against precomputed lookup attacks. If every user has a different random salt, the attacker cannot use one universal table against the whole database. The salt changes the input for each stored password hash.</p>
<p>This is why two users with the same password should still have different stored hashes. If the system uses a proper password hashing library, the salt is usually generated automatically and stored with the hash. Developers do not need to hide the salt. They need to make sure it is unique, random, and correctly included in the hashing process.</p>
<p>Rainbow tables are less central than they once were because attackers also use fast GPUs, cloud resources, and huge password lists. But the lesson remains useful. Unsalted fast hashes are weak because they allow reuse of work. Salted, slow, adaptive password hashing makes each guess more expensive and less reusable.</p>
<p>MD5 is especially poor for passwords because it is fast and widely precomputed. If a leaked database contains unsalted MD5 hashes, many common passwords may be identified quickly through existing lookup services. If the MD5 hashes are salted, lookup tables become less useful, but attackers can still run guesses with each known salt. That is still not enough protection.</p>
<p>Modern password storage should use Argon2id, bcrypt, scrypt, or PBKDF2 with appropriate settings. OWASP's <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a> gives current guidance. The specific algorithm matters, but the pattern is consistent: unique salts, cost parameters, tested libraries, and upgrade paths.</p>
<p>Salts also improve incident response. If every password hash has a unique salt and a modern work factor, defenders can more confidently tell users that stored passwords were protected with current practice. That does not remove the need for resets after some breaches, but it changes the scale and urgency of the risk.</p>
<p>A good test is to ask what an attacker can reuse. If the same hash appears for the same password across many users or sites, precomputed work becomes valuable. Unique salts and adaptive hashing reduce that reuse and force attackers into slower, account-by-account guessing.</p>
<p>The takeaway is not that every developer must study rainbow table construction in detail. The takeaway is that precomputation is a real attack pattern, and salts are a basic defense. If your system still stores unsalted MD5 hashes, treat it as urgent legacy debt and use <a href="/migrate-md5-password-hashes.html">How to Migrate Old MD5 Password Hashes Safely</a> as a starting point.</p>
